{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788976638,"nonce":"ofD0j1KvmVTjCJFhYwZBDB+3ocU=","message":"Labelled `perf-risk`, and it is the largest risk in the tracker. A\ngenerational collector needs a write barrier on every reference store, which\ntaxes the MUTATOR everywhere to make COLLECTION cheaper. binary_trees would win;\na benchmark that stores references and collects rarely could lose outright.\n\nOrder of work that keeps this honest: implement the barrier alone first, behind\na switch, and measure the corpus with it on and the collector unchanged. That\nisolates the tax from the benefit. Only build the sticky-mark-bit half if the\nbarrier's cost is small enough for the ~90ms of marking it would remove to pay\nfor it.\n\nCheaper alternative to try first: the GC trigger issue, which cuts marking by\nhalf with no barrier at all.","files":null}]}